eac3to-go: native eac3to for Linux and Windows
============================================

Native Blu-ray demuxer and audio converter for Linux and Windows, with the
familiar eac3to command-line syntax. Written in Go with native SSRC resampling
and Aften AC-3 encoding. Linux builds run without Wine or Windows DLLs.

An independent implementation targeting eac3to 4.00 behavior. Currently in
alpha: full compatibility is still being tested, and unsupported operations
fail explicitly. Linux amd64 and Windows amd64 packages are available.

Features
--------

- Inspect Blu-ray directories, MPLS playlists, containers and audio files.
- Extract video, audio, PGS subtitles and chapters; preserve encoded payloads
  during stream extraction, with metadata edits controlled by track options.
- Rank disc playlists using authored labels, navigation and clip metadata.
  Ambiguous cuts or episodes require an explicit playlist selection.
- Convert audio with gain, delay, downmixing, resampling and frame-rate changes.
- Extract DTS cores, Dolby companions and forced PGS captions.
- Use interactive track selection or explicit commands for scripts. JSON
  listings and extraction reports expose track metadata and residual mux delays.

Quick start
-----------

Download a Linux amd64 (.tar.gz) or Windows amd64 (.zip) package from:

  https://github.com/720pixel/eac3to-go/releases

Checksums and matching decoder source are included with each release. Extract
the archive and keep libexec/ beside eac3to or eac3to.exe. The Linux CLI is
static; its matched decoder helper requires glibc 2.35 or newer.

Install a matching FFmpeg/FFprobe pair. Blu-ray probing requires FFprobe built
with libbluray. Check the available tools and codecs with:

  ./eac3to --doctor

If the pair is missing, download it into an isolated user directory:

  ./eac3to --download-tools

This verifies the publisher's SHA-256 digest and checks both tools and Blu-ray
support. It leaves existing tools and PATH unchanged. To choose a new install
directory, add --tools-dir=/path/to/tools.

In Windows PowerShell, use the native executable:

  .\eac3to.exe --doctor
  .\eac3to.exe --download-tools

Explicit --ffmpeg=PATH and --ffprobe=PATH options take priority, followed by
EAC3TO_FFMPEG and EAC3TO_FFPROBE. Discovery also checks beside the executable,
tools/, PATH and the isolated user installation. The matched audio decoder
is found under libexec/ or on PATH; override it with --decoder=PATH or
EAC3TO_DECODER.

Usage
-----

The examples use eac3to on PATH. From the extracted directory, use ./eac3to
on Linux or .\eac3to.exe in Windows PowerShell. Replace the example paths
with your source and destination paths.

  # Select a playlist, tracks and output formats interactively.
  eac3to /media/disc/BDMV

  # List tracks without prompts, then extract selected tracks.
  eac3to movie.mkv --non-interactive
  eac3to movie.mkv 1: video.h264 2: audio.flac

  # Extract all tracks from a specific Blu-ray playlist.
  eac3to /media/disc/BDMV --playlist=00001 -demux -destpath=extracted

  # Convert audio or change its playback speed and apply a delay.
  eac3to input.wav output.flac
  eac3to input.wav output.wav -25.000 -changeTo24.000 +100ms

  # Extract a DTS core or forced PGS captions.
  eac3to movie.mkv 2: core.dts -core
  eac3to movie.mkv 3: forced.sup -forced

Track numbers come from the source listing; chapters occupy a number when
present. Track options follow the output filename. Options before the first
output apply to every output. Quote paths containing spaces, title selectors
such as '1)' and wildcard output names such as 'movie.*'.

Source-only commands open a wizard in interactive terminals. Use
--non-interactive for scripted listings. Commands with explicit outputs or
-demux run unattended; --interactive requests a review before processing.
--dry-run prints the plan. Existing outputs require --force. Temporary output
files are published only after processing succeeds.

--report=/path/to/extraction.json writes final output paths and remaining mux
delays after all outputs succeed. --playlist-video-window limits video to the
authored playlist in/out points while retaining audio framing and delay rules.
Run eac3to --help for the full option list.

Build and test
--------------

The Linux CLI needs Go 1.22+, C and C++ compilers, static runtime libraries
and make. Cgo is required for SSRC and Aften. The matched decoder build also
needs Linux amd64, Python 3.12+, GCC and git; it downloads pinned source
archives and builds under build/ without installing system tools.

  git clone https://github.com/720pixel/eac3to-go.git
  cd eac3to-go
  make build
  make decoder
  bin/eac3to --doctor
  make test
  make check

make windows builds Windows amd64 executables using an isolated, pinned MinGW
toolchain. See scripts/build-windows.py. FFmpeg/FFprobe remain separate runtime
dependencies on both platforms.

Compatibility
-------------

Tests cover generated format fixtures, encoded-byte comparisons, decoded PCM,
timestamps and damaged input. These checks do not establish complete eac3to
compatibility. Full-disc test media and local comparison records are excluded.
Windows runtime validation has been performed under Wine.

AC-3/E-AC-3 and lossy DTS decoding use the matched decoder helper, validated
against a bounded reference corpus. Strict TrueHD playlist starts and
unvalidated physical joins remain gated. --allow-unverified enables comparison
runs; it does not implement missing algorithms or establish output equivalence.

TrueHD and its embedded Dolby companion are extracted as separate elementary
files. A .thd output contains TrueHD only. Use -core with .ac3 or .eac3 to
extract the actual companion codec; no companion is generated for pure TrueHD.
Normal SSRC draining preserves the full filter tail; --reference-tail reproduces
the historical shorter tail. pcmcompare compares decoded samples, channel
layout, sample rate and length.

